            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text builds on the concept of addition by exploring a powerful shortcut. We recommend reviewing the previous chapters along with their prerequisites, as they provide the foundation for what we will discover here.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "introduction2",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn previous chapters, we learned to count by raising our fingers one at a time. This led us to add numbers step by step. But what happens when we have many things to count? Let's look at the hands in Figure 1 and think about a challenge:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left Hand</td><td>Right Hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Hands representations. Source code present in GitHub of the Project (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Código Fuente",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows a person's two hands raised with all fingers extended.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Counting in Groups</b><br /><br />Imagine three people standing together. We want to count all the fingers on their hands. Each person has 10 fingers (5 on each hand). How many fingers are there in total?<br /><br />Think about it before reading on.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "grouping2",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Grouping\n\n### A General Vision\n\nTo solve this problem, let's organize what we know in a table. This will help us see the big picture:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"tawapukllay\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">First Person</th><th colspan=\"2\">Second Person</th><th colspan=\"2\">Third Person</th><th>Total</th></tr>  <tr><td><img id=\"img1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img1', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"img2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img2', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"img3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img3', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"img4\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img4', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"img5\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img5', '0%')\" /></td><td><img id=\"img6\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img6', '0%')\" /></td><td><span class=\"text_to_paint\">?</span></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Representation of the hands in Reflection 1.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The table has six hands, each with five fingers raised.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We could count every finger one by one—that would work, but it would take a long time. Let's see how that would look:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi></math><br /><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>30</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Sum of all fingers.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Is There a Better Way?</b><br /><br />Calculation 1 looks very long because we have to write the number one thirty times! That is a lot of work.<br /><br />Can we find a shorter way to say the same thing?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Simplifying the Notation\n\nMathematicians faced this same problem centuries ago. Their solution was elegant: they created a special symbol called sigma (Σ) that means \"add the same number many times\"",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7b080e09-91f4-426d-8feb-c440249078d1",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>1</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>30 × (1)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>30</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Sum of all fingers using sigma notation.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Let's compare Calculation 1 and Calculation 2. The sigma symbol (Σ) replaces all those plus signs. The number at the bottom (n = 1) tells us where to start counting. The number at the top (30) tells us where to stop. The number to the right (1) is what we are adding each time. And the result? It is simply 30 times 1, which equals 30.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Can We Do Even Better?</b><br /><br />The sigma notation is shorter to write, but we still need to calculate 30 × 1. Is there an even simpler approach?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Grouping by Hands\n\nHere is a key insight: instead of counting fingers one by one, we can count by groups. Each hand has exactly 5 fingers. So instead of counting 30 individual fingers, we can count 5 fingers — six times (once for each hand). That is much faster!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>6 × (5)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>30</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Counting five fingers per hand.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Grouping by Person\n\nBut we can go even further! Instead of counting by hands, what if we count by person? Each person has 10 fingers (two hands of 5). Now we only need to add three times:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>10</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>10</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>10</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>10</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>3 × (10)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>30</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Counting ten fingers per person.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "nature",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Our Repetitions\n\nHumans are not the only ones who group things—nature does it too. And throughout history, people have created patterns based on repetition. Remember the Altar Q from Copán, Honduras? It shows generations of <i>ajawob</i> (kings), and we can use it to practice grouping.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "00f3ab60-2017-420a-99dc-56de384243e8",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Numerical families (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "40720958-0917-420e-8504-8c73be6da1bd",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Indigenous (Copan)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Slide 1</b>: Images of Altar Q at the archaeological site of Copán, Honduras. Photo taken during a visit on <htdate0>.<br />The names of the kings (<i>ajawob</i>) are detailed in the work of Anastasia Hutnick (<htcite0>).<br />The slide's source code was inspired by a template from (<htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "85c2d88c-6ad8-4858-95f8-0f157c49bf07",
                     "text": "Hutnick, Anastasia",
                     "page": "pp. 18, 35 - 67.",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2020",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cf7adc72-d340-412e-8d48-953ac64cb670",
                     "text": "W3Schools",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "-1",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "4",
                     "day": "26"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2020",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The altar is shaped like a big box, with inscriptions on top and sculpted human figures on its sides.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "On Altar Q, each of the four sides has exactly 4 kings carved into it. Instead of counting each king one by one, we can group them: 4 kings × 4 sides. Let's calculate the total:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"center_notation\"><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><munderover><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>4 × (4)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>16</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Total number of <i>ajawob</i> (reyes) per side of the altar.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Finding Groups Around You</b><br /><br />Look around you right now. Can you find objects that come in equal groups? Think about: egg cartons, windows on a building, tiles on a floor, or even the keys on a keyboard.<br /><br />How would you count them using groups instead of counting one by one?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "multiplication2",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplication\n\nEvery shortcut we have explored in this chapter—counting by hands, by person, or by groups of kings—is an example of multiplication. Mathematicians define it as",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fc77d792-25d3-4c13-ace0-0378f3aaec70",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"><li><i>\"...multiplication is the process of calculating the result when a number is repeated a specific number of times\"</i></li> </ul></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Notice that this definition does not mention addition directly. But as we have seen throughout this chapter, multiplication and addition are deeply connected. In fact, multiplication was invented precisely because adding the same number over and over becomes tedious. New mathematical tools are born from the need to solve problems more efficiently.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "final",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this chapter, we discovered that when we add the same number many times, there is a faster way: multiplication. We learned that grouping—by hands, by people, or by any equal set—turns a long addition problem into a simple multiplication.\n\nWe also saw that this idea is not new. Ancient civilizations used grouping and repetition in their art, architecture, and counting systems.\n\nIn the next chapter, we will continue exploring multiplication and see how it connects to the patterns we find in the world around us.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is it possible to add different numbers and get the same result?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "As we saw when we were counting our fingers, we could obtain the same number, 30, in different ways: Counting one by one, counting by hand (five fingers at a time), or counting by person (if multiple people are involved)."
      },
      {
         "question": "Is there a relationship between multiplication and addition?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Since multiplication is essentially repeated addition, there is an intrinsic relationship between these two mathematical operations."
      }
   ],
   "game_v2": null,
   "date_time": null
}
